<body>

<header>
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Amazon Bestselling Books: Price vs. User Rating Trend Analysis</h1>
    <div class="subtitle">A comprehensive analysis of how book pricing strategies relate to customer satisfaction across Fiction and Non-Fiction genres over a decade of Amazon bestseller data.</div>
    <div class="meta">Data Period: 2009–2019 &nbsp;|&nbsp; 550 Bestseller Entries &nbsp;|&nbsp; 351 Unique Titles &nbsp;|&nbsp; Prepared for Management Review</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="container">

<!-- KPI Cards -->
<div class="kpi-grid">
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="label">Average Book Price</div>
    <div class="value">$13.10</div>
    <div class="detail">Range: $0 – $105</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="label">Average User Rating</div>
    <div class="value">4.62</div>
    <div class="detail">Scale: 0–5 (range 3.3–4.9)</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="label">Price-Rating Correlation</div>
    <div class="value" style="color: var(--red);">−0.133</div>
    <div class="detail">Weak negative (Pearson r)</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="label">Avg. Reviews per Book</div>
    <div class="value">11,953</div>
    <div class="detail">6.57M total reviews</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="label">Genre Split</div>
    <div class="value">56% / 44%</div>
    <div class="detail">Non-Fiction / Fiction</div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Section 1: Market Overview -->
<div class="section" id="market-overview">
  <h2>1. Bestseller Market Overview (2009–2019)</h2>
  <p>This analysis covers <strong>550 bestseller entries</strong> representing <strong>351 unique book titles</strong> across 11 years of Amazon's annual top-50 lists. The dataset captures the dominant patterns in consumer book purchasing, with many perennial titles appearing in multiple years.</p>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <h3>Dataset Composition</h3>
      <table>
        <tr><td><strong>Total Entries</strong></td><td>550</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Unique Titles</strong></td><td>351</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Fiction Entries</strong></td><td>240 (43.6%)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Non-Fiction Entries</strong></td><td>310 (56.4%)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Years Covered</strong></td><td>2009 – 2019</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Books per Year</strong></td><td>50</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Genre Distribution by Year</h3>
      <div class="chart-container" style="max-height:280px;">
        <canvas id="genreStackChart"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box">
    <strong>Key Observation:</strong> Non-Fiction consistently dominates the bestseller list, holding a majority in 9 of the 11 years analyzed. The only year Fiction entries outnumbered Non-Fiction was <strong>2014</strong> (29 Fiction vs. 21 Non-Fiction), likely driven by franchise titles from Veronica Roth, John Green, and recurring children's classics.
  </div>

  <h3>Price Segment Distribution</h3>
  <p>The bulk of bestsellers are priced in the <strong>$6–$15 range</strong>, which accounts for 321 of 550 entries (58.4%). Premium-priced books ($31+) represent only 4.7% of bestseller entries.</p>
  <div class="chart-container" style="max-height:320px;">
    <canvas id="priceSegmentChart"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Section 2: Price-Rating Correlation -->
<div class="section" id="price-rating-correlation">
  <h2>2. Price vs. User Rating: Correlation Analysis</h2>
  <p>The central question of this report — whether higher prices correlate with higher customer satisfaction — is answered definitively by the data: <strong>they do not.</strong> In fact, the relationship is weakly negative.</p>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <div class="pearson-big">r = −0.133</div>
      <div class="pearson-label">Pearson Correlation Coefficient (Overall)</div>
      <table style="margin-top: 20px;">
        <tr><th>Segment</th><th>Pearson r</th></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="badge badge-fiction">Fiction</span></td><td><strong>−0.103</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="badge badge-nonfiction">Non-Fiction</span></td><td><strong>−0.135</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Overall</td><td><strong>−0.133</strong></td></tr>
      </table>
      <p style="margin-top: 16px; font-size: 14px;">The weak negative Pearson coefficient of <span class="stat-inline">−0.133</span> indicates that higher-priced bestsellers tend to receive <em>slightly lower</em> ratings — though the effect is small. This pattern holds across both genres, with Non-Fiction showing a marginally stronger negative correlation.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="chart-container" style="max-height:380px;">
        <canvas id="scatterChart"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Average Rating by Price Segment</h3>
  <div class="chart-container" style="max-height:320px;">
    <canvas id="segmentRatingChart"></canvas>
  </div>
  <div class="highlight-box">
    <strong>Interpretation:</strong> Free books ($0) and low-cost titles ($1–$5) achieve the highest average ratings (4.77 and 4.71, respectively). The $16–$20 segment shows the lowest average rating at 4.50. Notably, the $21–$30 range rebounds to 4.68 — driven by premium reference and gift books with dedicated audiences. Price does not buy satisfaction; if anything, value-priced books deliver stronger perceived quality.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Section 3: Genre Comparison -->
<div class="section" id="genre-comparison">
  <h2>3. Fiction vs. Non-Fiction: Pricing and Rating Strategies</h2>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-col" style="border-right: 3px solid var(--fiction); border-radius: 8px 0 0 8px; background: #f5f3ff;">
      <h4 class="fiction-color">Fiction</h4>
      <div class="compare-stat"><div class="cl">Entries</div><div class="cv fiction-color">240</div></div>
      <div class="compare-stat"><div class="cl">Avg. Price</div><div class="cv fiction-color">$10.85</div></div>
      <div class="compare-stat"><div class="cl">Avg. Rating</div><div class="cv fiction-color">4.65</div></div>
      <div class="compare-stat"><div class="cl">Avg. Reviews</div><div class="cv fiction-color">15,684</div></div>
      <div class="compare-stat"><div class="cl">Price-Rating r</div><div class="cv fiction-color">−0.103</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-divider"></div>
    <div class="compare-col" style="border-left: 3px solid var(--nonfiction); border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0; background: #fffbeb;">
      <h4 class="nonfiction-color">Non-Fiction</h4>
      <div class="compare-stat"><div class="cl">Entries</div><div class="cv nonfiction-color">310</div></div>
      <div class="compare-stat"><div class="cl">Avg. Price</div><div class="cv nonfiction-color">$14.84</div></div>
      <div class="compare-stat"><div class="cl">Avg. Rating</div><div class="cv nonfiction-color">4.60</div></div>
      <div class="compare-stat"><div class="cl">Avg. Reviews</div><div class="cv nonfiction-color">9,065</div></div>
      <div class="compare-stat"><div class="cl">Price-Rating r</div><div class="cv nonfiction-color">−0.135</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box">
    <strong>Key Finding:</strong> Fiction books on the bestseller list are priced <span class="stat-inline">$3.99 lower</span> on average than Non-Fiction, yet achieve <span class="stat-inline">higher ratings</span> (4.65 vs. 4.60) and <span class="stat-inline">73% more reviews</span> (15,684 vs. 9,065). Fiction delivers stronger perceived value at a lower price point. Non-Fiction's higher pricing may reflect reference materials (e.g., DSM-5 at $105, APA Manual at $46, SAT guides at $40) that serve utilitarian needs rather than enjoyment.
  </div>

  <h3>Genre Price Trends Over Time</h3>
  <div class="chart-container" style="max-height:380px;">
    <canvas id="genrePriceTrendChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Genre Rating Trends Over Time</h3>
  <div class="chart-container" style="max-height:380px;">
    <canvas id="genreRatingTrendChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <p>Fiction pricing remained relatively stable in the $9–$16 range, while Non-Fiction prices dropped dramatically from $17.62 (2011) to $10.57 (2019) — a <strong>40% decline</strong>. Meanwhile, Fiction ratings climbed steadily from 4.59 (2009) to 4.82 (2019), outpacing Non-Fiction's more modest rise from 4.58 to 4.69. The convergence in pricing post-2015, coupled with Fiction's rating ascent, suggests children's and young-adult titles — which are priced lower and rated higher — have increasingly dominated Fiction bestsellers in the second half of the decade.</p>
</div>

<!-- Section 4: Temporal Trends -->
<div class="section" id="temporal-trends">
  <h2>4. Year-over-Year Trends in Pricing and Ratings</h2>
  <p>This section examines how average pricing, customer ratings, and engagement volumes have shifted annually — the most critical lens for identifying inflection points in market dynamics.</p>

  <h3>Average Price and Rating Trend (2009–2019)</h3>
  <div class="chart-container chart-wide">
    <canvas id="priceRatingTrendChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Year</th><th>Avg. Price</th><th>Avg. Rating</th><th>Avg. Reviews</th><th>Fiction / Non-Fiction</th></tr>
    <tr><td>2009</td><td>$15.40</td><td>4.58</td><td>4,710</td><td>24 / 26</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2010</td><td>$13.48</td><td>4.56</td><td>5,480</td><td>20 / 30</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2011</td><td>$15.10</td><td>4.56</td><td>8,101</td><td>21 / 29</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2012</td><td>$15.30</td><td>4.53</td><td>13,091</td><td>21 / 29</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2013</td><td>$14.60</td><td>4.55</td><td>13,098</td><td>24 / 26</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2014</td><td>$14.64</td><td>4.62</td><td>15,860</td><td>29 / 21</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2015</td><td>$10.42</td><td>4.65</td><td>14,233</td><td>17 / 33</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2016</td><td>$13.18</td><td>4.68</td><td>14,196</td><td>19 / 31</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2017</td><td>$11.38</td><td>4.66</td><td>12,888</td><td>24 / 26</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2018</td><td>$10.52</td><td>4.67</td><td>13,930</td><td>21 / 29</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2019</td><td>$10.08</td><td>4.74</td><td>15,898</td><td>20 / 30</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Key Inflection Points</h3>

  <div class="rec-card">
    <div class="rec-title">2012: Rating Trough & Review Explosion</div>
    <div class="rec-body">Average ratings hit a decade low of <strong>4.53</strong> while review counts nearly tripled from the prior year (5,480 → 13,091). This coincides with the Fifty Shades of Grey phenomenon — a massively reviewed but polarizing title (3.8 rating) that dragged down the average while driving unprecedented engagement.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="rec-card">
    <div class="rec-title">2015: The Price Correction</div>
    <div class="rec-body">Average price dropped sharply from <strong>$14.64 to $10.42</strong> — a 29% year-over-year decline and the largest single-year price drop in the dataset. This coincides with the adult coloring book trend and a surge of affordable Non-Fiction titles. Non-Fiction's average price fell from $20.81 to $10.97 in a single year.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="rec-card">
    <div class="rec-title">2014: Fiction's Peak Representation</div>
    <div class="rec-body">Fiction claimed <strong>29 of 50 spots</strong> — its strongest showing in the decade. Franchise series (Divergent, The Fault in Our Stars, Gone Girl) and children's classics drove this spike. After 2014, Fiction representation subsided as Non-Fiction regained dominance.</div>
  </div>
