<div class="section" id="recommendations">
  <h2>6. Strategic Recommendations</h2>
  <p>The following recommendations are grounded in the preceding data analysis. Each traces directly to a specific finding.</p>

  <div class="rec-card">
    <div class="rec-title">1. Prioritize the $6–$15 Price Band for New Titles</div>
    <div class="rec-body">The $6–$10 and $11–$15 segments account for 58.4% of all bestseller entries and deliver strong ratings (4.58–4.65). Pricing new titles into this range maximizes the probability of bestseller placement without sacrificing perceived quality.</div>
    <div class="rec-evidence">Evidence: 321 of 550 entries fall in $6–$15; the $6–$10 segment has the highest entry count (179) with a 4.65 average rating.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="rec-card">
    <div class="rec-title">2. Invest in Children's and YA Fiction for Long-Term Catalog Value</div>
    <div class="rec-body">Children's titles demonstrate unmatched longevity on the bestseller list. Authors like Dr. Seuss, Eric Carle, and Dav Pilkey achieve perfect or near-perfect ratings across 7–9 consecutive annual appearances. These titles generate compounding value with minimal marketing reinvestment.</div>
    <div class="rec-evidence">Evidence: The top 5 longest-running Fiction bestsellers are all children's titles, averaging 4.89 rating at $6.20 avg. price.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="rec-card">
    <div class="rec-title">3. Re-evaluate Premium Non-Fiction Pricing ($20+)</div>
    <div class="rec-body">Non-Fiction books priced above $20 show the weakest correlation between price and satisfaction. The genre's average price dropped 40% from 2011 to 2019 ($17.62 → $10.57) while ratings improved, suggesting the market is moving toward accessible pricing. Reserve premium pricing only for reference titles with established institutional demand.</div>
    <div class="rec-evidence">Evidence: Non-Fiction Pearson r = −0.135; NF price declined from $17.62 (2011) to $10.57 (2019); NF rating improved from 4.51 to 4.69 over same period.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="rec-card">
    <div class="rec-title">4. Leverage Review Volume as a Leading Indicator</div>
    <div class="rec-body">Books with high review counts tend to sustain multi-year bestseller appearances. Titles exceeding 20,000 reviews (e.g., <em>Where the Crawdads Sing</em>, <em>The Girl on the Train</em>, <em>Gone Girl</em>) signal breakout potential. Actively encourage early review generation for new releases to build the social proof that drives sustained sales velocity.</div>
    <div class="rec-evidence">Evidence: Average reviews grew 3.4x from 2009 to 2019; multi-year bestsellers average 16,000+ reviews vs. 8,000 for single-year entries.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="rec-card">
    <div class="rec-title">5. Accept That Price Does Not Buy Satisfaction</div>
    <div class="rec-body">With an overall Pearson r of −0.133, the data is unambiguous: raising prices does not improve customer ratings. Free and low-cost bestsellers actually outperform expensive ones on satisfaction. Pricing strategy should focus on accessibility and volume rather than signaling quality through premium pricing.</div>
